## Generator input resolution

Generator-centered exploration accepts a small explicit input language.

Supported family aliases include:

```text
η / eta
ν / nu
σ / sigma
ι / iota
```

Indexed examples include:

```text
eta_2
nu_5
sigma_8
iota_4
```

Decorated aliases include:

```text
ν′
ν'
nu'
nu_prime

σ'
sigma_prime
σ''
sigma_double_prime
σ'''
sigma_triple_prime
```

Resolution produces an exact `GeneratorSymbol`. An unindexed family is not a wildcard.

## Top-level repository exploration

The repository-explicit entry point is:

```python
explore_repository_generator(
  repository,
  generator,
)
```

The production one-shot entry point is:

```python
explore_standard_repository_generator_input(
  generator_input,
)
```

This path searches registered repository-entry conclusions only.

Exploration is occurrence-based, not entry-deduplicated. Different structural paths remain distinct occurrences even when they occur in the same repository entry.

Unknown but syntactically valid indexed generators return a normal zero-occurrence report.

## Recursive proof-scope exploration

Phase 102 adds a second exploration path that searches the actual recursive `ProofStep.premises` ancestry below each registered production root.

The production entry point is:

```python
explore_standard_repository_generator_proof_scope_input(
  generator_input,
)
```

The path is:

```text
generator string
↓
resolve_generator_input()
↓
standard production repository
↓
registered root entries
↓
recursive ProofStep ancestry
↓
generator occurrences
↓
Toda membership extraction
↓
map-relation extraction
↓
RepositoryProofScopeExplorationResult
```

Each proof-scope node keeps:

```text
root_entry
proof_step
shortest_depth
```

Traversal is breadth-first, cycle-safe, identity-preserving, deterministic, and read-only.

Representative production results for `nu_prime` include

$$
\nu' \in \{\eta_3,2\iota_4,\eta_4\}_1
$$

and

$$
H(\nu')=\eta_5.
$$

The exploration layer only finds already represented and already proven statements. It does not evaluate $E$, $H$, or $\Delta$, and it does not solve Toda brackets.

## Applicable theorem / lemma discovery

Phase 103 adds read-only discovery of inference rules whose premise patterns are structurally compatible with statements found in the production proof scope.

The production entry point is:

```python
explore_standard_repository_generator_applicability_input(
  generator_input,
)
```

The command-line entry points are:

```powershell
python main.py explore-applicable nu_prime
python main.py explore-applicable nu_prime --detailed
```

The applicability path is:

```text
generator input
↓
proof-scope source statements
↓
premise-pattern compatibility
↓
applicability candidates
↓
rule groups
↓
rule families
↓
relevance-classified presentation
```

Applicability discovery does not execute a rule and does not establish a theorem conclusion.

Relevance categories are:

```text
THEOREM_SPECIFIC
MAP_PROPERTY
STRUCTURAL
BRIDGE
GENERIC_RELATION
UNCLASSIFIED
```

